{#
/**
 * @file
 * The organizer guide. The words are the approved copy, word for word.
 *
 * The h1 is the page title ("Organizer Guide", the route's title), so this
 * template has none. Reading width is 72ch (css/localnodes-guide.css). Each
 * of the six steps is an h2 inside the numbered list; the T-4 sections come
 * after it, outside the list.
 *
 * The last step, bringing in members, follows the stock invite screens as a
 * new member walks through them: the invite mail opens account creation, a
 * welcome mail signs them in, and the invitation waits under Invites in the
 * account menu.
 *
 * Available variables:
 * - show_fund: Whether to print "Your group's fund" (the site has
 *   localnodes_web3 on).
 * - show_fees: Whether to print "Network fees" (same condition).
 * - group_link: The community's group, as a link with its name, or NULL
 *   when there is none or this person can't open it.
 * - import_link: "Import tab", a link to the first group's Import tab or
 *   plain text.
 * - get_help_link: "Get help", a link to /help.
 * - assistant_name: The chat assistant's name, or NULL without the chat.
 * - members_link: "Manage members tab", a link to the first group's Manage
 *   members tab or plain text.
 * - fund_link: "Open the Fund tab", a link to the first group's Fund tab or
 *   plain text.
 * - get_help_url: The "Get help" page, /help.
 *
 * Link text names where the link goes, never "here".
 */
#}
<div class="localnodes-guide">
  <p class="localnodes-guide__intro">{% trans %}This guide takes you from your sign-in link to your first answer with sources, then to your members reading and asking.{% endtrans %}</p>

  <ol class="localnodes-guide__steps">
    <li>
      <h2>{% trans %}Set your password{% endtrans %}</h2>
      <p>{% trans %}Open the sign-in link in your welcome email. Choose Log in. Set your password, then choose Save.{% endtrans %}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>{% trans %}Open your group{% endtrans %}</h2>
      {% if group_link %}
        <p>{% trans %}Your community already has a group named after it: {{ group_link }}. Open it. You can rename it, or make more groups, whenever you like.{% endtrans %}</p>
      {% else %}
        <p>{% trans %}Your group isn't ready yet. {{ get_help_link }}.{% endtrans %}</p>
      {% endif %}
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>{% trans %}Upload your first files{% endtrans %}</h2>
      <p>{% trans %}To add documents, open your group's {{ import_link }} and choose Upload files. Choose your files, tick the box that says you have the right to share them, and press the Import button. Use files whose text you can select and copy: the assistant can't read image-only scans, and a very short file may be too short to use.{% endtrans %}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>{% trans %}Wait while your documents are prepared{% endtrans %}</h2>
      <p>{% trans %}The "Preparing documents for the assistant's search" panel on the Import tab shows how many are prepared, for example "18 of 21". This happens in the background. You can leave the page and come back.{% endtrans %}</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>{% trans %}Ask the AI assistant a question{% endtrans %}</h2>
      {% if assistant_name %}
        <p>{% trans %}Go to your group's page. The AI assistant, called "{{ assistant_name }}", is at the bottom right. It isn't shown on the Import tab. It looks for answers in the pages you can see. Ask something your documents answer. When the assistant finds an answer it can give with sources, open a numbered source to check it. Otherwise read the documents or ask for help.{% endtrans %}</p>
      {% else %}
        <p>{% trans %}Go to your group's page. The AI assistant is at the bottom right. It isn't shown on the Import tab. It looks for answers in the pages you can see. Ask something your documents answer. When the assistant finds an answer it can give with sources, open a numbered source to check it. Otherwise read the documents or ask for help.{% endtrans %}</p>
      {% endif %}
    </li>
    <li>
      <h2>{% trans %}Bring in your members{% endtrans %}</h2>
      <p>{% trans %}To invite people, open your group's {{ members_link }}, choose Add members, then Invite users. Enter their email addresses, or their names if they are already on the site, and press "Send your invite(s) by email".{% endtrans %}</p>
      <p>{% trans %}Each person gets an email. Someone new to the site creates an account from it and follows the sign-in link in the welcome email. After setting a password and choosing Save, they see their pending invitations. They choose Accept for your group, then Join if asked. Someone who already has an account can sign in and open Invites from their profile menu.{% endtrans %}</p>
      <p>{% trans %}Your members read the documents in the group's Topics, where each original file can be opened. To ask, they go to the group's page and use the AI assistant at the bottom right. It looks for answers in the pages each member can see.{% endtrans %}</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  {% if show_fund %}
    <section class="localnodes-guide__section" aria-labelledby="localnodes-guide-fund">
      <h2 id="localnodes-guide-fund">{% trans %}Your group's fund{% endtrans %}</h2>
      <p>{% trans %}Each group can have a fund: money the group holds together. A group organizer sets it up from the group's Fund tab. Money leaves the fund only when members vote for it and enough of them agree. {{ fund_link }} to set it up.{% endtrans %}</p>
    </section>
  {% endif %}

  {% if show_fees %}
    <section class="localnodes-guide__section" aria-labelledby="localnodes-guide-fees">
      <h2 id="localnodes-guide-fees">{% trans %}Network fees{% endtrans %}</h2>
      <p>{% trans %}Some actions in a fund, such as a vote, have a small network fee. Your community pays these fees, so members never do.{% endtrans %}</p>
    </section>
  {% endif %}

  <section class="localnodes-guide__section" aria-labelledby="localnodes-guide-assistant">
    <h2 id="localnodes-guide-assistant">{% trans %}Using the assistant{% endtrans %}</h2>
    <p>{% trans %}Ask the assistant about anything in your community's files and posts, for example "When is the next meeting?" or "What did we decide about the budget?". When the assistant finds an answer it can give with sources, open a numbered source to check it. Otherwise read the documents or ask for help. It only sees what you're allowed to see: two people asking the same question can get different answers. It cannot act for the community, open your email inbox or browse other websites; it can read permitted library text, including an email address written in a document.{% endtrans %}</p>
  </section>

  <p class="localnodes-guide__help">{% trans %}Stuck? <a href="{{ get_help_url }}">Get help</a> is in the footer of every page.{% endtrans %}</p>
</div>
